pattern: an escaped character ends a range like any other

The check for a reversed range in a bracket expression looked at the
character right after the '-'. When the end of the range was escaped,
that was the backslash, so the check compared against '\' instead of
the escaped character and let a reversed range through:

	Regexp(`[0-\ ]`, 0)  // "(?s)[0- ]"

regexp.Compile rejects that with "invalid character class range: `0- `",
although Regexp documents that a nil error means the result compiles.
The escaped character is now the end of the range, so the pattern fails
with "invalid range: 0- " as [z-a] does; an escaped ']' ends a range
too, rather than being mistaken for the end of the bracket expression.
FuzzRegexp found it; the input is kept as a seed.

Reproducer, before this change: regexp.MustCompile(Regexp(`[0-\ ]`, 0))
panicked.

diff --git a/pattern/pattern.go b/pattern/pattern.go
index 81c9610..837db02 100644
--- a/pattern/pattern.go
+++ b/pattern/pattern.go
@@ -336,8 +336,14 @@ func regexpNext(sb *strings.Builder, sl *stringLexer, mode Mode) error {
 				bsb.WriteByte('-')
 				start := sl.last()
 				end := sl.peekNext()
+				// An escaped end, as in [0-\ ], is the character after
+				// the backslash; it ends the range even when it is ']'.
+				escaped := end == '\\' && len(sl.peekRest()) > 1
+				if escaped {
+					end, _ = utf8.DecodeRuneInString(sl.peekRest()[1:])
+				}
 				// TODO: what about overlapping ranges, like: [a--z]
-				if end != ']' && start > end && deferredErr == nil {
+				if (escaped || end != ']') && start > end && deferredErr == nil {
 					deferredErr = &SyntaxError{msg: fmt.Sprintf("invalid range: %c-%c", start, end)}
 				}
 			case ']':
